1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,380
 In this video, we'll be talking about arrays, how to create an access arrays, and how to

2
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:11,020
 iterate over arrays.

3
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:14,700
 Arrays are a way to store multiple pieces of the same kind of data.

4
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:21,000
 When you're working with an array, the data is stored consecutively in an array of data.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,740
 Each piece of data in the array is called an element.

6
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:28,340
 To access items in the array, an array index is used.

7
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:34,000
 The index always starts at zero, meaning zero items from the array start.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,940
 The zero index is simply multiplied by the size of the items.

9
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:42,220
 So at index zero, you're accessing the very beginning, and index one, you're multiplying

10
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:48,960
 the size of the item by one, meaning you skip over the very first item, ending up on

11
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:50,440
 item two.

12
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:55,120
 So zero is item one, one is item two, et cetera.

13
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:59,320
 Lastly, arrays are a fixed size and cannot be resized.

14
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:03,160
 Go does provide facilities, however, to work with arrays of different sizes, and we'll

15
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:09,120
 be taking a look at those in a future video.

16
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:13,920
 So here's a visualization of an array in memory.

17
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:20,280
 If we have this structure right here, a discount card structure, having a ID, store code,

18
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:23,800
 and a rate, this is what it would look like in memory.

19
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:31,240
 So here we have blocks of memory, and the ID takes up two bytes here.

20
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:37,040
 The store code takes up one byte, and the rate takes up four bytes.

21
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:41,280
 This entire chunk here is one discount card structure.

22
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:47,400
 And when you put them into an array, you'll have one here, one here, and one here.

23
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:54,120
 So when we're dealing with array indexes, we have index zero, which is zero bytes from the

24
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:55,120
 beginning.

25
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:56,120
 So it starts right here.

26
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:58,960
 And each one of these discount cards is seven bytes.

27
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:03,840
 So if we want to access item number three, we have to use index two.

28
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:09,880
 So it'll take seven bytes, multiply it by two, which is 14, and it's going to skip over

29
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:15,800
 14 bytes, and you'll end up exactly at your item number three by using index number

30
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:18,080
 two.

31
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:24,040
 So this top one is how it looks in memory, and you can also visualize it like this.

32
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:30,400
 So here we have the item zero, one, two, three, four.

33
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:38,520
 It's the same thing, just a different way to visualize it.

34
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:44,760
 So to create an array, we use the var keyword followed by the name of the array, we have square

35
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:50,040
 braces, the number of items that we want within our array, and the type of the items that

36
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:52,000
 are going to be in the array.

37
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,080
 So this first example is uninitialized.

38
00:02:55,080 --> 00:03:01,560
 So we'll have an array of three integers, and they'll all be equal to zero.

39
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:07,040
 In this second example, we're using the create an assign, we're using the square braces

40
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:13,360
 to make an array, and again, we're making three integers, and we use curly braces to set

41
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:15,240
 the initial values for these integers.

42
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:19,200
 So we're going to have three integers, and they're going to be equal to seven, eight,

43
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:21,300
 and nine in that order.

44
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:26,680
 Here on our third example, we're using the create an assign again, square braces for an

45
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:28,720
 array, and three dots.

46
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:33,080
 So what three dots is going to do is these dots are going to look over here and see

47
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:37,000
 how many items you've created, and it's just going to substitute that number here for

48
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:38,000
 you.

49
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,880
 So the third way is kind of like the default way you'd want to make an array.

50
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:47,360
 That way, you can easily update the number of items without having to actually worry

51
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:50,320
 about how many what your number is right there.

52
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:55,800
 So this will be three and seven, eight, and nine will be your integers.

53
00:03:55,800 --> 00:04:00,400
 And this last one here is again, create an assign, we're actually creating a size for

54
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:03,160
 array containing integers again.

55
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:08,160
 The first ones will be seven, eight, and nine, and the fourth item will be zero.

56
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:09,640
 So the fourth will get a default.

57
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:16,120
 So it's we only supplied three numbers.

58
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:21,400
 So to access array elements, we take the name of the array, my array in this case, we use

59
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:28,720
 the square braces, the index that we want to access in equal sign, and the value that we

60
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:29,920
 want to assign.

61
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:32,080
 So this will be how we assign data.

62
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:33,600
 So these three will assign.

63
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:38,120
 So we can index zero, one, and two will be seven, eight, and nine, just like our previous

64
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:39,440
 slide.

65
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:44,320
 And then here on the bottom, if we want to read some data, we can just take the name of

66
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:48,360
 the array, our square braces, and the index that we want to read.

67
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:52,160
 So really just depends if you want to assign, then your array variable will be on the left

68
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:53,160
 side.

69
00:04:53,160 --> 00:05:00,640
 If you want to read, then your array variable will be on the right side.

70
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:02,200
 We can also iterate through an array.

71
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:06,040
 So that just means we're going to go through each item one at a time.

72
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:10,640
 To do so, we use the four loops, since that's our go to loop and go.

73
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:12,560
 And what you'll do is you'll have your array.

74
00:05:12,560 --> 00:05:17,840
 So we create one here, we have three elements automatically calculated based on the numbers

75
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:18,840
 supplied.

76
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:22,520
 Here on the bottom, we have our four loop.

77
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:25,720
 We create iteration variable i, assign it to zero.

78
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:30,000
 As long as i is less than this function, we're going to go into the loop body, and then

79
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:35,160
 continue on with our typical plus plus to increment it by one.

80
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:39,240
 So this function here is the length functions, len for short.

81
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:44,080
 And what that does is it goes and calculates how many values are in this array.

82
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:49,840
 So for this particular one, it's going to be three because we have three values in our array.

83
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:53,800
 So whenever you're iterating through arrays, you normally want to use the lin or length

84
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:57,280
 function in order to find out how many you've working with that way you can iterate

85
00:05:57,280 --> 00:05:58,280
 through each one.

86
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:03,680
 What we're doing in this loop is we're reading from the array since the arrays on the

87
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:08,160
 right side of the variable, and we're assigning a copy into this item variable.

88
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:14,200
 So when we do this, we're making a copy from this array into item.

89
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:19,320
 We then just print it out.

90
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:23,200
 So it's really important when you're working with arrays that you stick within the bounds

91
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:24,240
 of your array.

92
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:29,040
 That means if you have an array with three items and you try to access a fourth item, then

93
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:30,440
 you're going to get an error.

94
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:34,780
 Let's take a look at a few situations where you get some errors.

95
00:06:34,780 --> 00:06:37,960
 This one on the left side, this will be a runtime error.

96
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:43,000
 So here we have an uninitialized array that has a size of three, and all the integers

97
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:44,560
 will be zero.

98
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:48,960
 In our for loop, we're not checking the length of the array here.

99
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:53,400
 We're just saying that, hey, keep looping as long as i's less than 10, and we're trying

100
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:59,480
 to print out my array and then the iteration index.

101
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:05,400
 Now it'll happen here is zero, one, and two, those will all check out, because we have

102
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:06,720
 three items.

103
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:11,400
 Once we get to i equals to four, then the program's going to crash because there's no

104
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:15,360
 data at that point because there's only three in the array.

105
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:20,280
 So this right here is going to be a runtime error, and this is why you'll almost always

106
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:25,320
 want to use the length function as part of your condition check.

107
00:07:25,320 --> 00:07:28,800
 On the right hand side here, we'll get a compile time error.

108
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:33,520
 So again, we're just creating an array that has three integers, and we're setting the

109
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:38,920
 first three to be seven, eight, and nine, and then we're setting the fourth value to be

110
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:39,920
 10.

111
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:44,120
 However, there's only three values in the array, not four.

112
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:47,280
 And so you're going to get an error when you try to compile the program, and it's able

113
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:49,440
 to calculate how many are in the array.

114
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:54,760
 And since it's reading all the information in your source code, it knows that at this

115
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:59,240
 point there's only three, and at this point you're trying to access a fourth, and so that's

116
00:07:59,240 --> 00:08:01,640
 just going to be a compiler error.

117
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:05,320
 On the left side, it's not able to make that determination ahead of time, so it happens

118
00:08:05,320 --> 00:08:06,320
 at runtime.

119
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:10,240
 And I hear on the bottom, we have the same as the above.

120
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:15,600
 So we're just using the assign equals instead of the index directly.

121
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:20,040
 So we're trying to read from the fourth index, which doesn't exist, and that's going

122
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:24,680
 to be compile time error as well.

123
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:30,440
 Let's recap, arrays are a fixed size collection of same type items, arrays are accessed

124
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:33,160
 using an array index.

125
00:08:33,160 --> 00:08:37,160
 It is an error to use an index that's outside the bounds of an array.

126
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:41,800
 Array elements can be optionally set during array creation, and any elements that you don't

127
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:45,560
 a set during creation will have a default value assigned.

128
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:50,440
 You can use the LEN function, which is short for length, to get the length of an array

129
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:59,160
 and use it to iterate in a for loop.

130
00:08:59,160 --> 00:08:59,880
 You'll almost always want to use this function when you're iterating an array.

131
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:01,840
 That way you don't get any out of bounds errors.

